THE ONE MISS, AND WHY IT DOES NOT REHABILITATE THE OLD ORACLE.  probe4_M_GLUEPROMPT.raw: `disasm lines 2, commands owe
3', `prompt lines 20, commands owe 19' -- and in exactly ONE read session.  That is the survive session, the only one
that steps TWO instructions (`mov.l @r1,r2' then the marker store): the newline-less `GXemul> ' printed during the
first instruction landed in front of the SECOND instruction's disassembly line, turning a disassembly line into a
prompt line, so the two counts moved in opposite directions.  In the eleven single-step read sessions the glue landed on
the next prompt line exactly as Fable derived, and the count oracle passed every one of them.  So the kill was an
accident of one session's shape -- a detector that happened to step twice somewhere -- not a property of the oracle;
removing that one two-instruction session would have made Fable's prediction exact.  Recorded as Fable asked ("If
either is killed, my hold on that leg is wrong and I want it recorded"): the hold on M_GLUEPROMPT was wrong AS A
PREDICTION ABOUT THE 35-ROW INSTRUMENT, and right about the class.
